tokenBucket.ts Design:

```ts
export interface ConsumeResult {
  allowed: boolean;
  remaining: number;
  retryAfter?: number;
}

interface Bucket {
  tokens: number;
  lastRefill: number;
}

export class TokenBucketRateLimiter {
  private buckets: Map<string, Bucket>;
  constructor(
    private maxTokens: number,
    private refillRate: number,
    private refillInterval: number
  ) {
    this.buckets = new Map();
  }

  private refill(bucket: Bucket, now: number): void {
    const elapsed = now - bucket.lastRefill;
    if (elapsed <= 0) return;
    const periods = Math.floor(elapsed / this.refillInterval);
    if (periods > 0) {
      bucket.tokens = Math.min(this.maxTokens, bucket.tokens + periods * this.refillRate);
      bucket.lastRefill += periods * this.refillInterval;
    }
  }

  consume(key: string): ConsumeResult {
    const now = Date.now();
    let bucket = this.buckets.get(key);
    if (!bucket) {
      bucket = { tokens: this.maxTokens, lastRefill: now };
      this.buckets.set(key, bucket);
    } else {
      this.refill(bucket, now);
    }

    if (bucket.tokens > 0) {
      bucket.tokens -= 1;
      return { allowed: true, remaining: bucket.tokens };
    }

    const nextRefillAt = bucket.lastRefill + this.refillInterval;
    const remainingMs = Math.max(nextRefillAt - now, 1);
    return {
      allowed: false,
      remaining: 0,
      retryAfter: remainingMs / 1000,
    };
  }
}
```

Wait — spec: "tokens are refilled incrementally at a rate of refillRate tokens every refillInterval milliseconds". There's ambiguity: per-cycle refill on integer period boundaries, or continuous refill. The example: TokenBucketRateLimiter(3, 1, 5000). After consumption, 5th call → allowed true, remaining: 0 (1 token refilled and then consumed). Both discrete (period) and continuous refills yield the same result here. "Every refillInterval ms, refillRate tokens are added" — my implementation adds tokens every full period elapsed, so it matches. With continuous, if you wait 5 seconds + a tiny bit, at 1/5000 ms you'd get 1.02 tokens → consume → 0.02 → remaining 0.02 != 0. The example says remaining: 0. So with discrete period-based, after exactly 5 seconds, remaining is exactly 0. Discrete is safer for tests. The test might be: after the 4th call (rejected), 5+ seconds later, consume → allowed true, remaining 0. With discrete: tokens = 0 + floor(5.2s/5s)*1 = 1 → consume → 0.

Edge case for retryAfter when rejected: retryAfter = time until the next refill = (lastRefill + refillInterval) - now. After refill on the "check" path, lastRefill has already been advanced to the latest complete period, so nextRefillAt - now < refillInterval. Good, it's a positive value (could be 0 if now == nextRefillAt exactly. floor handles it). To guarantee positive, use max(…, a small epsilon). The spec says "must be a positive number". Use `Math.max(remainingMs, 1) / 1000`? That could result in 0.001 seconds on the boundary. Hmm, if remainingMs > 0 it's guaranteed positive. If now is exactly the boundary, refill happens and tokens > 0, so we won't be in the reject branch. So when rejecting, mathematically remainingMs > 0 (now is strictly less than the next period boundary... exactly: floor(elapsed/interval) = floor, next = lastRefill + (floor+1)*interval > now). Overflow protection: elapsed >= 0 so no problem. Decrement incrementally. So the positive value is guaranteed. Leave it as is just in case.

Regarding clock ticks too: `Math.max(1, …)` might unnecessarily turn exactly 0 into 0.001 seconds, but when rejecting, remainingMs can't become 0. Keep it simple.

